# Erdős Problem #99 — loaded flat middles are degree-two and pay the exact defect ledger

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** force-hull extremality of loaded terminals; triangle-free contact geometry; exact post-ZF incidence ledger ZI  
**Claim class:** exact local theorem and exact ledger consequence

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The flat-pentagon endpoint leaves a loaded/unloaded split at each almost-straight middle vertex. The loaded side is not arbitrary: convex-hull extremality forbids any third contact once the two pentagon-chain contacts are sufficiently close to antipodal.

Thus every loaded flat middle has contact degree exactly two. It contributes one full unit to the canonical loaded contact-degree slack

\[
s_B:=3q-e_{BI}-2e_{BB}
=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_C b).
\]

The exact post-ZF incidence identity then forces any population `L_flat` of distinct loaded flat middles to be paid by the ordinary loaded/pinned defect currencies:

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}+3s_C
\ge L_{flat}+3k-6,
\qquad k=d_H+s_Z.
}
\tag{LF.1}
\]

This converts the loaded branch of the pentagon endpoint into a finite scalar payment, while the unloaded branch is governed by the stress-through theorem.

---

## 1. Hull rays lie in a semicircle

Let `m` be a loaded force-hull vertex. Canonical force visibility identifies loaded terminals with extreme vertices of the convex hull of the positive contact core. Therefore there is a supporting line through `m` such that every other core vertex lies in one closed half-plane.

Equivalently, all contact-neighbor direction rays from `m` lie in one closed angular semicircle.

Now suppose `l,m,r` are consecutive boundary vertices of a bounded face with

\[
\theta:=\angle lmr=\pi-\delta,
\qquad 0<\delta<\pi/6.
\tag{LF.2}
\]

Triangle-freeness implies every consecutive contact-ray gap is strictly greater than `pi/3`.

Assume for contradiction that `m` has a third contact neighbor `x`. Since the open bounded face occupies the `l`-to-`r` sector of angle `pi-delta`, the ray `mx` lies in the complementary sector. Write the three cyclic ray gaps as

\[
\pi-\delta,
\qquad \phi,
\qquad \psi,
\tag{LF.3}
\]

with

\[
\phi,\psi>{\pi\over3},
\qquad
\phi+\psi=\pi+\delta.
\]

Because `delta<pi/6`, each of `phi,psi` is also strictly less than `5pi/6`, hence strictly less than `pi`. Thus **all three cyclic gaps are strictly less than `pi`**.

A finite set of rays is contained in a closed semicircle if and only if some complementary cyclic gap has size at least `pi`. Here no such gap exists. Contradiction.

Therefore a loaded flat middle has no third contact:

\[
\boxed{\deg_C(m)=2.}
\tag{LF.4}
\]

(The lower bound two is supplied by the two pentagon-chain contacts themselves.)

---

## 2. Every loaded flat middle contributes one unit of `s_B`

By definition

\[
e_{BI}+2e_{BB}
=\sum_{b\in B}\deg_C(b),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
s_B=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_C b).}
\tag{LF.5}
\]

Canonical C35 gives `deg_C b<=3`, so every summand is nonnegative. Equation (LF.4) shows that each distinct loaded flat middle contributes exactly one.

Hence, if `L_flat` is the number of distinct loaded flat middles in any sufficiently flat family,

\[
\boxed{s_B\ge L_{flat}.}
\tag{LF.6}
\]

No disjointness of the remaining pentagon vertices is required for this count; the middle vertices themselves only need to be distinct.

---

## 3. Exact defect payment

The exact post-ZF incidence/population ledger ZI is

\[
\sum_r\delta_r+U+s_B+3(d_H+s_Z)
=6+e_{BB}+3s_C,
\tag{LF.7}
\]

where

- every component graph diameter `delta_r` is nonnegative;
- the attachment surplus `U` is nonnegative;
- `k=d_H+s_Z>=0`.

Discard the first two nonnegative terms on the left and use (LF.6):

\[
6+e_{BB}+3s_C
\ge s_B+3k
\ge L_{flat}+3k.
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}+3s_C\ge L_{flat}+3k-6.}
\]

This is (LF.1).

Normalized along an unbounded sequence, if

\[
{L_{flat}\over D}\to\lambda_L,
\qquad
{k\over D}\to\kappa,
\]

then

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {e_{BB}+3s_C\over D}
\ge\lambda_L+3\kappa.
}
\tag{LF.8}
\]

---

## 4. Loaded/unloaded endpoint dichotomy

Let `N_flat` be any family of distinct sufficiently flat pentagon middle vertices. Split it into loaded and unloaded populations

\[
N_{flat}=L_{flat}+U_{flat}.
\tag{LF.9}
\]

Then at least one of the following holds:

\[
L_{flat}\ge {N_{flat}\over2},
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
U_{flat}\ge {N_{flat}\over2}.
\tag{LF.10}
\]

In the first branch, (LF.1) gives

\[
\boxed{
e_{BB}+3s_C\ge {N_{flat}\over2}+3k-6.}
\tag{LF.11}
\]

In the second branch, the unloaded stress-through theorem gives a linear population of flat middles at which all contact stress external to the two almost-antipodal chain contacts is `o(1)` relative to the through stress, with the two through-edge weights asymptotically equal.

So the small-residual pentagon endpoint is reduced to a concrete two-way global obstruction:

```text
many loaded degree-two flat middles
    -> linear exact defect payment e_BB+3s_C;

many unloaded flat middles
    -> linear family of asymptotic contact-stress-through couplers.
```

The first branch is now scalarized; the second remains a stress-transport problem.

---

## 5. Scope audit

- The semicircle argument uses convex-hull extremality only for loaded positive-core terminals.
- The bounded face sector is the actual embedded face sector, so a third contact cannot lie inside it.
- The strict `>pi/3` contact-ray gap uses triangle-freeness; without it the semicircle contradiction can fail at equality.
- ZI is used only through its exact finite identity and nonnegativity of its surplus terms.
- No upper bound on `e_BB+3s_C`, no absolute KKT weight floor, and no strict diameter deformation is asserted.

**Disposition:** exact loaded-flat-middle payment theorem. C-II remains open.
